WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:04.340
Sometimes, all it takes is one role to change
your career.

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:09.890
This is especially true of Seann William Scott,
who became a huge star from "American Pie."

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:15.320
His role as the crude, rude, dim-witted dude
Stifler gained him enough notoriety that would

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:17.101
impact his career forever.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:23.560
Before "American Pie," Seann William Scott,
himself, was not a particularly noteworthy

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:24.560
performer.

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:29.289
He had zero feature film credits to his name,
and his most notable forays into performing

00:00:29.289 --> 00:00:32.759
were appearing in a Sunny Delight commercial
and an Aerosmith video.

00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:37.860
At age 28, he was working at the Home Depot
and going on lots of auditions — and it

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:41.760
was that conviction that eventually resulted
in "American Pie."

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:46.680
Part of his early struggles can be attributed
to how Scott initially saw himself.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:48.329
Scott told NBC in 2008,

00:00:48.329 --> 00:00:51.040
"I always anticipated having a career in sports.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:53.019
[But] movies were my passion.

00:00:53.019 --> 00:00:57.310
[...] I was reminded that I could have a life
outside of being a stressed out professional

00:00:57.310 --> 00:00:58.310
athlete.

00:00:58.310 --> 00:01:02.390
I realized I could use the same work ethic
and apply it to another career that I love.

00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:07.020
I graduated early, told my coaches I wasn't
going to play sports anymore, and moved to

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:08.020
LA.

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:09.820
I got lucky, got a manager, and that was that."

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:15.479
When Scott was first hired for the role of
Stifler, it hadn't been written as a major

00:01:15.479 --> 00:01:16.479
character.

00:01:16.479 --> 00:01:19.889
In fact, Stifler only had a passing presence
in the story.

00:01:19.889 --> 00:01:21.399
Scott told “Entertainment Weekly” in 2019,

00:01:21.399 --> 00:01:27.840
"The Stifler character was in two or three
scenes and he was written to be such an a**hole

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:31.039
that I was like, 'I don't even know if I'd
want to play this guy.’

00:01:31.039 --> 00:01:35.770
So I was like, 'I'm going to rewrite a bit
of it, make him the guy you hate to love.'

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:37.439
He's probably insecure.

00:01:37.439 --> 00:01:40.039
He's like the guy who wants to be loved the
most."

00:01:40.039 --> 00:01:44.560
As it would turn out, his meager scripted
presence in the production resulted in a mediocre

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:45.560
payday.

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:51.489
Despite portraying a fan favorite character
in the sex comedy, Scott only scored $8,000

00:01:51.489 --> 00:01:52.489
for playing the role.

00:01:53.489 --> 00:01:58.460
The summer of 1999 began with moviegoers counting
down the days to the first new "Star Wars"

00:01:58.460 --> 00:01:59.919
movie in 16 years.

00:01:59.919 --> 00:02:04.890
By the end of that season, many had shifted
to discussions of the over-the-top raunch

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:06.249
of "American Pie."

00:02:06.249 --> 00:02:11.989
A good deal of this talk involved Seann William
Scott, who embodied a classic version of abrasive

00:02:11.989 --> 00:02:16.010
masculine immaturity, and whose commitment
to the part turned what could have been a

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:18.390
nuisance into a fan favorite.

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:23.590
Scott's appeal as Stifler was so strong that
it even lasted well into the various "Pie"

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:24.590
sequels.

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:29.469
While reviewing 2012's "American Reunion,"
Roger Ebert mentioned his affinity for Scott's

00:02:29.469 --> 00:02:32.260
Stifler while calling him a highlight of the
film.

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:33.260
Ebert shared,

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:38.129
"Seann William Scott [...] has made the role
of Stifler his own, and seems able to morph

00:02:38.129 --> 00:02:44.230
his face into an entirely new person [...] Narrowed
eyes, broad maniacal grin, frightening focus,

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:47.860
still with all the zeal for seduction and
adventure he had in high school.

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:52.049
The ingenuity with which he destroys the jet
skis of two jerks can only be admired."

00:02:52.049 --> 00:02:57.420
Even in these more enlightened times, a 2019
retrospective review of the original "American

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:02.060
Pie" that criticized the feature's politically
incorrect elements still found time to praise

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:03.780
Scott's performance.

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:08.109
Years removed from when "American Pie" first
hit theaters, Scott can still drum up enough

00:03:08.109 --> 00:03:12.209
praise to remind everyone why he became the
film's biggest breakout star.

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:18.209
In the wake of "American Pie," Scott's status
as an actor was significantly elevated.

00:03:18.209 --> 00:03:23.370
His first appearance, in 2000's original "Final
Destination," was a brief one that had him

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:25.700
meeting a graphic demise on-screen.

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:30.230
After that, Scott headlined the Todd Phillips
feature "Road Trip," another raunchy comedy

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:34.900
that cast him among a group of students who
hit the road in pursuit of sexual delights.

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:39.660
Other roles around this time included "Dude,
Where's My Car?" and "Evolution", all playing

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:43.340
off the dim, horny jock persona he suddenly
had all to himself.

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:48.579
It's an age-old Hollywood question: Do you
lean into your notoriety and risk the diminishing

00:03:48.579 --> 00:03:49.690
returns of repetition?

00:03:49.690 --> 00:03:55.110
Or do you try to blaze a unique path, and
risk alienating your newfound fans?

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:58.709
Scott shared in 2003 about his struggle with
such questions, saying,

00:03:58.709 --> 00:04:02.690
"I was like ‘nah, being the funny guy, that's
not why I came to LA.’

00:04:02.690 --> 00:04:06.629
But I appreciated it, and I realized that
this is really just a blessing to be able

00:04:06.629 --> 00:04:07.629
to make people laugh."

00:04:08.819 --> 00:04:13.719
When a comedy becomes as successful as "American
Pie," it's only a matter of time before talk

00:04:13.719 --> 00:04:14.810
turns to a sequel.

00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:17.759
"Pie" presented a pair, almost immediately.

00:04:17.759 --> 00:04:22.049
The first of these arrived in 2001 under the
name "American Pie 2."

00:04:22.049 --> 00:04:27.690
A smash hit, its success got "American Wedding"
greenlit, which would arrive in 2003.

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:33.200
Suddenly, the franchise wasn't about teenage
sexual escapades anymore, but instead, watching

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:36.120
the characters navigate various stages of
young adulthood.

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:40.530
Through it all, Stifler remained an anchor
of immaturity who could always be counted

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:44.509
on for crazy antics and ill-advised situations.

00:04:44.509 --> 00:04:45.610
Scott admitted in 2003,

00:04:45.610 --> 00:04:47.940
"I don't know why people like the character.

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:50.690
[It's because of] the way [his friends] played
off that character.

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:54.551
Because, if they would have dismissed him
and just treated him as an utter a**hole [...] as

00:04:54.551 --> 00:04:57.660
an audience member [you’d be] like, 'Whoa,
he's just a jerk.'

00:04:57.660 --> 00:05:00.140
[...] [But] they just play with him, and they
accept what he does."

00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:02.850
“You’re a very unpleasant individual,
you know that?”

00:05:03.850 --> 00:05:09.539
While films like 2001's "Jay and Silent Bob
Strike Back" and 2003's "Old School" cast

00:05:09.539 --> 00:05:14.150
Scott in supporting roles, he also got plenty
of opportunities to become a leading man.

00:05:14.150 --> 00:05:20.110
In 2003, he was cast opposite fellow-rising-star
Dwayne "The Rock" Johnson in Peter Berg's

00:05:20.110 --> 00:05:21.780
so-so "The Rundown."

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:27.069
He also signed on for 2005's high-profile
reboot of "The Dukes of Hazzard."

00:05:27.069 --> 00:05:32.300
Despite a massive ad campaign, "Dukes" bombed
both critically and at the box office.

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:37.900
A string of ill-conceived comedies followed,
many trying to recapture that Stifler magic.

00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:44.360
2007's "American Loser" cast him opposite
Gretchen Mol as a rudderless adolescent adult.

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:48.800
That same year, "Mr. Woodcock" put him opposite
Billy Bob Thornton as an adult looking to

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:50.770
get revenge for his adolescence.

00:05:50.770 --> 00:05:55.699
2008's "The Promotion" had Scott competing
with John C. Reilly to get past the adolescent

00:05:55.699 --> 00:05:58.650
ways that had stopped them from advancing
in their careers.

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:05.139
In 2008, however, Scott made a dent with Paul
Rudd on one of his first star vehicles, "Role

00:06:05.139 --> 00:06:06.139
Models."

00:06:06.139 --> 00:06:10.319
Trying to put some space between his "Models"
character and Stifler, Scott said at the time,

00:06:10.319 --> 00:06:12.409
"He's not really mean to anybody.

00:06:12.409 --> 00:06:14.889
He's actually a very optimistic guy.

00:06:14.889 --> 00:06:19.150
But clearly [...] the whole [...] physicality
of it, and [...] that vibe is very similar.

00:06:19.150 --> 00:06:22.720
But, I had wanted to add something that made
him a little more irreverent."

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:27.259
"Role Models" earned significantly better
reviews than "Hazzard" and Scott's other films

00:06:27.259 --> 00:06:30.860
of this period, and also fared better at the
box office.

00:06:30.860 --> 00:06:35.080
But still, now a decade after his "American
Pie" breakthrough, Seann William Scott was

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:37.970
still trying to overcome the same type casting
limitations.

00:06:39.259 --> 00:06:43.800
Like many other comedic actors before him,
Seann William Scott tried to branch out with

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:45.360
more serious films.

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:50.129
It was a smart endeavor, but, unfortunately,
it seems he may have just bet on the wrong

00:06:50.129 --> 00:06:51.129
horses.

00:06:51.129 --> 00:06:55.900
His big, high-profile leading man play was
undoubtedly "Bulletproof Monk."

00:06:55.900 --> 00:06:58.520
The 2003 film was based on a comic book.

00:06:58.520 --> 00:07:04.270
It was also the directorial debut of in-demand
music video director Paul Hunter and starred

00:07:04.270 --> 00:07:05.800
Chow Yun-Fat.

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:10.720
This was a high-profile action film that had
potential to launch a franchise, with Seann's

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:14.310
character as its Luke Skywalker-like hero-in-training.

00:07:14.310 --> 00:07:15.659
What could go wrong?

00:07:15.659 --> 00:07:20.080
To answer that question, ask yourself: When
was the last time you heard anyone talk about

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:21.610
"Bulletproof Monk?"

00:07:21.610 --> 00:07:26.080
Around the same time, he signed up for what
was a very in-demand movie — "Southland

00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:27.080
Tales."

00:07:27.080 --> 00:07:32.500
It was filmmaker Richard Kelly's feverishly-anticipated
follow-up to "Donnie Darko," a movie that

00:07:32.500 --> 00:07:36.259
seemed to have announced the arrival of Hollywood's
newest auteur.

00:07:36.259 --> 00:07:41.289
Unfortunately, the production became notoriously
troubled, the film was rumored to be largely

00:07:41.289 --> 00:07:45.979
incoherent, and after years of delays was
released with little fanfare.

00:07:45.979 --> 00:07:50.389
In more recent years, Scott has appeared in
the grisly thriller "Bloodline" and the crime

00:07:50.389 --> 00:07:53.770
drama "Already Gone," but neither of them
fared much better.

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:58.960
In 2010, Seann William Scott kicked off a
new decade by connecting with one of the most

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:02.180
prolific names in 1990s indie cinema.

00:08:02.180 --> 00:08:07.530
Filmmaker Kevin Smith probably seemed like
a safe bet, as did starring opposite the legendary

00:08:07.530 --> 00:08:09.000
Bruce Willis.

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:13.159
What Scott most likely didn't anticipate,
however, was a troubled production that had

00:08:13.159 --> 00:08:17.949
the original title dropped against Smith's
wishes, the director and leading man coming

00:08:17.949 --> 00:08:23.090
to despise each other, and the entire experience
being so miserable that Smith hasn't made

00:08:23.090 --> 00:08:25.330
a studio film since.

00:08:25.330 --> 00:08:29.199
Scott was, however, one of the funnier parts
of the finished film.

00:08:29.199 --> 00:08:34.100
Despite "Cop Out" faring poorly with critics
and the box office, it did earn the actor

00:08:34.100 --> 00:08:36.500
a dream role — well, maybe.

00:08:36.500 --> 00:08:41.460
Smith even considered his collaboration with
Scott to be the inspiration he needed to push

00:08:41.460 --> 00:08:46.570
"Hit Somebody," a hockey comedy based on a
Warren Zevon song, across the goal line.

00:08:46.570 --> 00:08:48.250
In 2010 Smith explained,

00:08:48.250 --> 00:08:50.900
"Seann, for me, was the key into the character.

00:08:50.900 --> 00:08:54.140
This dude has something in him that you can
just see hangs so well on Buddy.

00:08:54.140 --> 00:08:56.160
He's got some pain that's going to work out
well."

00:08:56.160 --> 00:09:00.330
As you may have guessed, "Hit Somebody" has
still not made the leap from the page to the

00:09:00.330 --> 00:09:01.430
silver screen.

00:09:01.430 --> 00:09:06.020
It was going to be two movies, then one movie
again, then a mini-series, and eventually

00:09:06.020 --> 00:09:10.030
Smith just read the script on his podcast
in 2018.

00:09:10.030 --> 00:09:14.580
Whether the film is ever resurrected — and
whether Scott will still play the part — seems

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:16.300
doubtful at this point.

00:09:16.300 --> 00:09:20.500
Fortunately, if you want to see Scott in the
world of hockey, we still have his two “Goon”

00:09:20.500 --> 00:09:21.500
films.

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:26.870
In 2013, Seann William Scott returned to the
role that had made him a household name with

00:09:26.870 --> 00:09:28.440
"American Reunion."

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:32.660
It was the fourth official installment of
the franchise and had the "American Pie" gang

00:09:32.660 --> 00:09:34.470
return for more raunchy escapades.

00:09:34.470 --> 00:09:39.200
This gave Scott the opportunity to return
to the role of Stifler, now a decade older

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:41.080
but only marginally wiser.

00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:42.080
“Yeah!

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:43.630
That’s perfect.”

00:09:43.630 --> 00:09:47.170
Scott didn't need any compulsion to come back
to the franchise that made him.

00:09:47.170 --> 00:09:49.440
In fact, according to him, it was his idea.

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:50.440
He shared,

00:09:50.440 --> 00:09:54.170
"It was a long time in the making because
I pitched the movie three years ago.

00:09:54.170 --> 00:09:56.660
The idea of bringing everybody back for a
high school reunion.

00:09:56.660 --> 00:10:02.420
I [said] three years to go, 'Wow, how much
fun is it going to be to play Stifler in his

00:10:02.420 --> 00:10:03.420
30s'.

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:04.420
It was a blast."

00:10:05.420 --> 00:10:09.380
Two decades after his "American Pie" breakthrough,
Scott is still a working actor — although

00:10:09.380 --> 00:10:14.170
it's hard to argue that any of his several
dozen roles has come close to Stifler as far

00:10:14.170 --> 00:10:16.320
as mass appeal and awareness.

00:10:16.320 --> 00:10:21.040
In 2018, he took over the lead role in the
FOX cop show "Lethal Weapon."

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:25.610
Though he was coming on to a program fraught
with behind-the-scenes drama, Scott's presence

00:10:25.610 --> 00:10:29.390
on the show gave it a much-needed positive
boost in publicity.

00:10:29.390 --> 00:10:34.250
Intriguingly, Scott apparently pursued the
role himself once he saw that the show's original

00:10:34.250 --> 00:10:36.840
lead, Clayne Crawford, had been fired by the
program.

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:41.420
Though "Lethal Weapon" with Scott in the lead
role lasted just one season, that wasn't the

00:10:41.420 --> 00:10:42.600
end of his TV work.

00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:48.350
Later in 2021, he'll be back on the FOX network
with "Welcome to Flatch," a docu-comedy that

00:10:48.350 --> 00:10:50.980
aims to be "The Office" for small-town America.

00:10:50.980 --> 00:10:56.420
A comedic project, "Flatch" seems like it
could represent a fundamental shift in Scott's

00:10:56.420 --> 00:11:00.630
career, as he plays an authority figure in
the lives of some younger troublemakers.

00:11:00.630 --> 00:11:04.910
Perhaps this is the beginning of Sean William
Scott reinventing himself as a middle-aged

00:11:04.910 --> 00:11:07.440
actor whose days of frivolity are behind him.

00:11:08.440 --> 00:11:13.070
It has now been well over two decades since
Scott's life was changed forever through the

00:11:13.070 --> 00:11:14.970
antics of Stifler in "American Pie."

00:11:14.970 --> 00:11:19.340
Though it has had its ups and downs, he has
insisted that he has no regrets about the

00:11:19.340 --> 00:11:21.170
career-launching role.

00:11:21.170 --> 00:11:22.310
Scott said in 2018,

00:11:22.310 --> 00:11:25.680
"I didn't think Martin Scorsese was going
to cast me in his next movie.

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:28.170
I understood why I wasn't getting cast for
other things.

00:11:28.170 --> 00:11:29.590
I would have been a distraction.

00:11:29.590 --> 00:11:34.620
I kind of pigeonholed myself, but I felt really
lucky to get the chance to make people laugh."

00:11:34.620 --> 00:11:39.640
Given that the combined worldwide grosses
of his movies have exceeded $1 billion, it

00:11:39.640 --> 00:11:42.670
is hard to feel too bad for Seann William
Scott.

00:11:42.670 --> 00:11:45.300
Some gambles have paid off, others have not.

00:11:45.300 --> 00:11:49.840
But talk to any actor, and they'll most likely
tell you they'd be happy to have a decades-long

00:11:49.840 --> 00:11:53.540
career with more than 30 TV and film projects
under their belt.

00:11:53.540 --> 00:11:55.500
As Scott himself put it in 2018,

00:11:55.500 --> 00:11:57.910
"I just got lucky with 'American Pie.’

00:11:57.910 --> 00:12:00.230
That movie gave me a career."

